A||||7||BBBAD GT||84 vs 87 Elec Rad Fan||||5||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||08-29-2000||05:09 PM||||From what I've read, I think the 84 fan was a duel speed unit, while maybe the rest of the years was a single speed.  Is this correct?  The 87 fan is obviously bigger than the 84 (comparing my two Fieros).  <P>Any info on the differences between the two is greatly appreciated, because I have the 84 (weaker by appearence) fan cooling the V8.  It does the job ok, but the temps does creep up standing still in traffic on a hot hot day (4 core rad to be pushing air through).  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fsrowsell||08-29-2000||07:33 PM||||I had the same problem with my 84. So I bought a Perma-Cool fan part#19114...<BR>What a Fan!http://www.permacool.com/Catalog/Cat_page16.html<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||08-29-2000||07:40 PM||||All 84's with AC came with a 2 speed fan.  That's the only difference I know of..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-29-2000||11:50 PM||||I think if you take it frame and all the 87 fits. can't say for sure.<P>on 2 speed cars..... for the wire you just connect both hot wires from the 2 speed car harness to the one hot wire on the fan. make sure you get this right. If you reverse the polarity of the fan you'll spin it backwards and have problems.<P>You should also consider one of the growing list of hood scoop options to get rid of the hot air better.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||08-30-2000||09:48 AM||||I'm not too concerned about when the car is moving, just when stuck in traffic.  Here is a pic of my current setup, see the holes forward of the battery...there are corresponding holes on the other side of the compartment to vent down.  There are also holes in the shroud, design to flap open when air is pushing through it from moving.  This was Chris's last minute mod before driving 500 miles in the 90+ heat for the exchange.  It did run approx 10 degrees cooler, without touching the hood (I just drove 500 miles over last weekend, never went over 190 with AC going).  I'm thinking of twisting the battery around, and putting metal dryer ducting from the front hole to the back hole, so when it rains, it wouldn't get that compartment all messy.  BTW, there isn't enough space in the 87 shroud to cut a hole like that, telling me the fan is bigger (the motor is obviously bigger). Any comments?<P> <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/cooling.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/cooling2.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-30-2000||03:36 PM||||For a start I'd get some gommets on the holes where the battery cables go thru. You could end up with a major short if you don't. (At least they look like they are passing thru metal.)<P>How many wires go to the fan electrical connector. 2 wires is single speed. 3 is double speed. If you have the 2 speed fan you need to figure out if it's on low or high. If it's running on low, which it does most of the time, then you need to get it running on high. The easiest way to do that is bypass the resistor on the fan frame.<P>The 87 may be bigger but it may not flow enough more air to solve your problem. You may need to go aftermarket. Those are not only bigger but the fans likely have more flow rate.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||BBBAD GT||08-30-2000||04:12 PM||||That piece that the cables go through is just a plastic piece, not metal.  No worries there.  <P>I'll look at the wires tonight, I bet its on high when its on (he's got a temp switch hooked up to it through the rad).  I really don't have a major problem, I was just wondering about the differences between the two, because they physically are so much different.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-31-2000||03:12 PM||||If you already have one it certainly won't hurt to try it. I think the 87 fan is 5 blades the 84 is 4. That would increase flow. I don't know how much.||||reg||1
